    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">Tāufaʻāhau</a>, baptised as George, ended half a century of civil war and united Tonga as King George Tupou I in 1845–1852.</li><li>His 1862 code freed commoners from forced service to chiefs, and his 1875 constitution, still in force, forbade the sale of land.</li><li>A British protected state from 1900 to 1970 but never a colony, Tonga remains a monarchy under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupou-vi">Tupou VI</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Tonga regained full independence on 4 June 1970, the 108th anniversary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">George Tupou I</a>’s 1862 code freeing commoners from forced service to their chiefs.</p></aside>
    </section>

